          ],
          "text": "On admission his oxygen saturations were 90% on room air, respiratory rate was 58, and he had mild-moderate subcostal recession with widespread fine creps and wheeze. Nasopharyngeal aspirate confirmed RSV. He was commenced on supplemental oxygen to maintain saturations ≥90% and nasogastric feeds (IV fluids available if not tolerated). No antibiotics were given given the viral aetiology, and salbutamol was not used as it is not indicated in bronchiolitis. By Day 2 his saturations had improved to 93–95% on room air with a respiratory rate of 44 and only mild recession; he was weaned off supplemental oxygen overnight and the nasogastric tube was removed once he was taking approximately 75% of feeds orally.",
